1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,463
Most meetings repeat, and most meetings have people.

2
00:00:04,463 --> 00:00:09,001
This video shows how to make an event repeat daily, weekly, monthly or

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,599
yearly, how to change or cancel one occurrence or the whole series, how

4
00:00:13,599 --> 00:00:18,495
to invite people and guests, how invited people answer, and how status, busy

5
00:00:18,495 --> 00:00:21,660
or free, and visibility change what others see.

6
00:00:26,261 --> 00:00:31,079
Add an event as usual, then look at the Repeat section of the editor.

7
00:00:31,079 --> 00:00:36,668
Repeats offers Does not repeat, Daily, Weekly, Monthly and Yearly, and every says

8
00:00:36,668 --> 00:00:39,880
how many days, weeks, months or years apart.

9
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:42,771
Here a coaching session repeats weekly.

10
00:00:43,222 --> 00:00:47,992
A weekly event repeats on the days you tick, here Tuesday and Thursday.

11
00:00:47,992 --> 00:00:52,019
A monthly event repeats on the same day of the month, or on

12
00:00:52,019 --> 00:00:56,232
the same weekday of the same week, such as the second Tuesday.

13
00:00:56,682 --> 00:01:01,381
Under Ends, choose Never, a date, or a number of occurrences.

14
00:01:01,381 --> 00:01:03,625
This one ends after eight.

15
00:01:03,625 --> 00:01:08,745
For a pattern these choices don't cover, a custom rule, which is an

16
00:01:08,745 --> 00:01:11,550
iCalendar repeat rule, takes over.

17
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:12,812
Choose Save.

18
00:01:13,264 --> 00:01:17,446
The series shows on every Tuesday and Thursday it covers.

19
00:01:17,446 --> 00:01:20,964
The Month view shows the whole run at a glance.

20
00:01:25,164 --> 00:01:29,130
Open any occurrence of a repeating event and choose Edit.

21
00:01:29,130 --> 00:01:33,915
The editor asks you to apply the changes, or the cancellation, to This

22
00:01:33,915 --> 00:01:37,504
occurrence, This and following, or All occurrences.

23
00:01:37,955 --> 00:01:42,342
Keep This occurrence and change the time, and only that day moves.

24
00:01:42,342 --> 00:01:45,085
The rest of the series stays as it was.

25
00:01:45,535 --> 00:01:50,122
Choose This and following to change a day and every day after it.

26
00:01:50,122 --> 00:01:55,355
The series ends before that day, and a new series starts with your changes.

27
00:01:55,806 --> 00:02:00,979
To cancel one day, open the occurrence, keep This occurrence, and choose Cancel.

28
00:02:00,979 --> 00:02:03,805
Just that day disappears from the series.

29
00:02:03,805 --> 00:02:07,594
Choosing All occurrences cancels the whole event instead.

30
00:02:07,594 --> 00:02:12,466
To change how the event repeats, edit the series from its first occurrence.

31
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:20,243
The Attendees section of the editor invites people.

32
00:02:20,243 --> 00:02:25,073
Under People, choose Invite users, search by name, and pick one or more

33
00:02:25,073 --> 00:02:26,578
users of the site.

34
00:02:26,578 --> 00:02:30,467
Here Maria invites Jamie and Sam to a customer workshop.

35
00:02:37,911 --> 00:02:40,492
Guests are people outside the site.

36
00:02:40,492 --> 00:02:44,711
Type one per line, as an email address, or as a name followed

37
00:02:44,711 --> 00:02:47,167
by the address in angle brackets.

38
00:02:47,167 --> 00:02:52,771
With Calendar Invitations turned on, people with an email address are also emailed.

39
00:02:53,221 --> 00:02:55,970
After saving, open the event again.

40
00:02:55,970 --> 00:03:01,668
Answers lists everyone invited, with a check mark for accepted, a question mark

41
00:03:01,668 --> 00:03:06,831
for maybe, a cross for declined, and an empty circle for no answer yet.

42
00:03:11,021 --> 00:03:13,600
Jamie opens the event he was invited to.

43
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:17,581
At the top the editor says You are invited, and shows his current

44
00:03:17,581 --> 00:03:19,881
answer, which he has not given yet.

45
00:03:20,332 --> 00:03:24,076
Accept, Maybe and Decline record his answer at once.

46
00:03:24,076 --> 00:03:28,530
The Answers list shows it, and so it does for the organizer and

47
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:31,112
everyone else who opens the event.

48
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:38,422
Status is Confirmed, Tentative or Cancelled.

49
00:03:38,422 --> 00:03:42,231
Show as is Busy, or Free for an event that is shown but

50
00:03:42,231 --> 00:03:45,603
leaves the time free, such as an optional lunch.

51
00:03:45,603 --> 00:03:50,660
A free event doesn't count when people look for a time that suits everyone.

52
00:03:51,111 --> 00:03:55,010
Visibility is Same as the calendar, Public, or Private.

53
00:03:55,010 --> 00:03:59,802
For a private event, others who can only see the calendar's busy time

54
00:03:59,802 --> 00:04:03,061
see that the time is taken, and nothing else.

55
00:04:03,512 --> 00:04:07,561
Some kinds of events need approval, such as time off on a calendar

56
00:04:07,561 --> 00:04:09,304
that another module adds.

57
00:04:09,304 --> 00:04:13,972
They are created as Tentative, and someone who manages the calendar opens the

58
00:04:13,972 --> 00:04:15,772
event and chooses Confirm.

59
00:04:17,772 --> 00:04:21,642
Repeat an event on the days you choose, change one day or the

60
00:04:21,642 --> 00:04:25,800
rest of the series, invite people and guests, and let them answer.

61
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:27,302
Thanks for watching.

